std.intrinsics:ò
alloc*äAllocates room for `count` values of `T`, zero-filled.

Returns a null pointer when `count` is 0. An allocation that cannot be
satisfied terminates the process rather than returning null â€” there is no
failure channel here yet.:ü
resize*íResizes a block from `old_count` to `new_count` values of `T`, preserving
the first `min(old_count, new_count)` values and zeroing any growth.

`p` must have come from `alloc`/`resize` at `old_count`, or be null (in
which case this allocates). A `new_count` of 0 frees and returns null.

The returned pointer may differ from `p`; the old one must not be used
again.:©
free*œReleases a block obtained from `alloc`/`resize`. A null `p` is a no-op.

`count` must be the element count the block was last sized at. This does
*not* run `drop` on the values in the block â€” scope-exit `drop` (see
`spec/todo.md` item 6) does not recurse through a container's elements,
only through struct fields â€” so a collection holding resource-owning
elements leaks them regardless of what it does here.:‘
copy*„Copies `count` values of `T` from `src` to `dst`.

The two ranges must not overlap. Written as an element loop rather than a
bulk byte move because a bulk primitive would be one more intrinsic, and
this is not on a path where its cost has been shown to matter.